🧠 Basic Commands I Learned

echo

Prints output to the terminal.

echo hello

Also used to write into files:
echo “text” > file.txt

⸻

whoami

Tells me which user I am currently logged in as.

whoami

⸻

ls

Lists files and directories.

ls
ls -la   (shows hidden files and details)

⸻

cd

Used to navigate between directories.

cd foldername
cd ..   (go back)

⸻

cat

Displays file content.

cat file.txt

⸻

pwd

Shows current directory path.

pwd

(I initially confused this as “pdw” but it’s pwd)

⸻

find

Used to search for files.

find / -name file.txt

⸻

grep

Searches for specific text inside files.

grep “word” file.txt

⸻

Recursive grep

grep -r “word” .

Searches in all files inside a directory.

⸻

## Shell Operators

AND operator (&&)

Runs second command only if first is successful.

mkdir test && cd test

⸻

> (overwrite)

Writes output to a file (overwrites existing content).

echo hello > file.txt

⸻

>> (append)

Adds output to file without removing existing content.

echo world >> file.txt

⸻

## User & Privileges

id

Shows user ID, group ID, and privileges.

id

⸻

sudo

Runs command with elevated privileges (as root).

sudo apt update

⸻

Root User

Root = highest privilege user in Linux.

What root can do:

* Access all files
* Install/remove anything
* Modify system settings
* Control other users

⸻

How to check if I am root

whoami

If output is:
root

Then I have full privileges.

Or:

id

If UID = 0 → root user

⸻

##  How This Connects to Penetration Testing

File Discovery

* ls, find → locating important files
* Useful for finding configs, passwords, hidden files

⸻

Reading Sensitive Data

* cat → reading files like config files, logs, credentials

⸻

Searching Data

* grep → finding keywords like password, admin, token

⸻

Privilege Escalation

* Checking user with whoami
* Using sudo to gain higher privileges
* Goal is often to become root

⸻

Command Chaining

* Using && to execute multiple steps efficiently
* Helpful in automation during attacks

⸻

